For air: #244 open, auto-merge armed (job f94546, per your 4fd9ff). Every step and its evidence is in the body:
- the drive is recorded under TP-476 0-C as the read half;
- every figure was re-derived from the raw streams (which caught a substring-scoring defect in my own first matrix; re-scored, it agrees run for run);
- both reviewers ran, with no blocker, and one fix batch followed;
- full tier: parallel 22108 passed and 1 failed (the Air's Apple git record-merge red), serial 1938 passed;
- archive probe 179 passed; audit clean; landing check clean.

The claim sat at eight sites, not six: sandbox_posture.py:44 and TROUBLESHOOTING:514 said "has not been driven yet".

One correction, mine: "the docs say read globs expand to concrete paths when the session starts" was my inference written as the docs' word. The settings reference says only "On Linux and WSL2, Claude Code expands a read entry to the concrete paths it matches"; it does not say when. The record now quotes it with the read date.

Two candidates, yours to route:
(1) A row owning the undriven read surface, with its own oracle: Linux and WSL2 (forms, the allowRead narrowing, present and late files), the PowerShell tool's reads, other read commands (the three rows' own spellings), and a rename or link of a denied file before the read (a sandboxed write to a denied dotenv lands, so a rename probably does too). Plus a drive kept in the repo: the driver and re-scorer committed, or ported into bench/end_to_end. My driver lives only in this session's scratchpad.
(2) docs/HOOKS.md:1383 (win-2's): "a secret read on a host with no sandbox to refuse it" implies every sandboxed host refuses one. That holds only with the posture written and the retry off, and is driven on macOS only.

Next: job a509c2 (DEF-5003, DEF-5004). Both gates have merged (#242, #243), so it starts now from origin/main.

For air, re a509c2 (DEF-5003, DEF-5004), before I claim. Both gates have merged. On df355d6 the probes read the sites as filed: 5 callers of clone_machine; 7 raw reads in 5 functions. The plan, from the assign and the nearest code:
- clone_machine returns (name, how), sharing machine_setting's reason grammar;
- each caller says which: read_sessions marks the clone's sessions unread; block_key gives the failed read as its note; seats' note says so; resolve and cmd_retire refuse without the naming advice;
- _seat_claims_kept keeps the worktree;
- _own_name says the read failed;
- worktree_name_inherited answers False on a failed read, as its docstring promises;
- name_this_worktree says the read failed at the clone read and at the other worktree's read; the read-back keeps its refusal, with a marker;
- _record_fragments._seat refuses rather than label the fragment local;
- the earn-the-red is a config read that times out, and one git cannot parse.

One conflict outside my files: two test files sit under win's live-tree-remainder claims.
(1) tests/test_board.py (616f77): read_sessions is tested only there. I need one new test for the clone's failed read, a hunk of its own in the read_sessions class.
(2) tests/test_contracts.py (30442b): to strike both rows with something holding them at 0, I'd extend my own TestEveryNameReaderSaysWhichItIs class (#242) to the clone_machine callers and the raw reads, through the same predicate in tests/_name_readers.py. A hunk inside my class, far from win's sites. A new contract file instead needs a _MARKER_RULES line in conftest.py, also win's.

Options:
(a, recommended) both hunks in my lane, the way 50f438 let win-2 touch test_write_guard.py; the second to merge runs catch-up if git ever reads a conflict;
(b) I build everything else now and add those two hunks after win's lane merges;
(c) strike on the probes alone, with no pin (not recommended: a new raw read would re-open the class silently).

Holding all edits until you answer.

